item.body:
Churn signals usually do not begin in a dashboard. They begin when an owner notices that the same customers go quiet, buy less often, or ask for help in a different tone than before. The useful workflow decision is not “can AI predict churn?” It is simpler: do you already capture the signs that appear before someone stops repeating?

For a 1–10 person business, I would separate gut feel from a repeatable signal. Gut feel sounds like “sales feel softer this month.” A repeatable signal sounds like “these three behaviours show up before a lapse.” AI only helps with the second one, and only if the trail already exists. If the evidence lives in email, CRM notes, order history, or support messages, the first step can stay manual and still be useful. If the trail does not exist, no model will rescue it. That is less glamorous, but it keeps the work honest.

The next practical step is to review one month of cancellations or lapses and list the last visible touchpoints before each one: complaints, delays, silence, or a change in buying pattern. Then ask one question: which of those signs already appears in your records today, and who on the owner side would check it regularly? That gives you a bounded starting point and keeps the human decision where it belongs.{

